                <pb facs="0531" n="523" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg523"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.516/684: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-05-27T22:00:00+09:00">May 27, 1940—10
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received May 27—9:42 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>383. Continuing my 382.</p>
                <p>3. With regard to paragraph 2 of the Department’s telegram 141, May 2, 7 p.m., in
                    view of the events in Europe and of their possible consequences at present
                    monopolizing attention in Japan and hindering clarification of Japanese foreign
                    policy, and with reference to Shanghai’s 415, May 14, noon, to the
                        Department,<anchor n="96" xml:id="d555-f1"/> I cannot suggest any further
                    steps which could be usefully taken at this time. We shall continue to follow
                    the situation closely and make future recommendations if and when further action
                    may appear helpful or necessary.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
